; worldprops.eval - the world's properties, areas and doorways (W4-W6).
;
; RUN IT THROUGH tools\WorldTest.py, NOT ON ITS OWN. It WRITES to the
; project - `levels new` creates a level and saves the manifest - and the
; restore lives in WorldTest's finally block, not here. Run bare, it leaves
; the manifest naming a level whose files are then cleaned up from under it,
; and the next check reports a project that cannot reach it. (Which is how
; this note came to be written.)
;
; Everything here was hand-edited in world.map or project.ini until now. Three
; things are worth watching:
;
;   - AREA ORDER IS A RULE, not a presentation detail: they may overlap and the
;     LAST row wins. So a new area APPENDS (an exception carved out of a broad
;     region wins, which is what carving one means), and reordering is an
;     operation rather than a sort.
;   - The world's START and the GAME'S OPENING are different facts in different
;     files. The party's start is where it stands when it begins on the map;
;     the opening may be a dungeon instead.
;   - Refusals are the rules the LOADER asserts on, enforced early: a duplicate
;     id, an occupied cell, impassable ground. Each probe is an `expect-refuse`
;     line: it must be refused, and a refusal anywhere else fails the run
;     (code-review C442 - these used to pass only because they PRINTED).
logecho on
reset
worldedit on

; --- the properties, as they stand ------------------------------------------
worldprops

; --- the start refuses impassable ground (0,0 is water) ---------------------
expect-refuse worldprops start 0 0
worldprops start 7 6
worldprops

; --- locations: add, refuse a duplicate, move, retarget, delete -------------
; crypt_gate stands at 10,10. It used to stand at 12,7, and when it moved the
; two moves below swapped roles without a word: the "real" move was refused,
; the "onto a doorway" one went through, and the occupied-cell add became a
; second duplicate-id add - all of it printed, none of it counted, until the
; refusals did (code-review C442).
worldloc
worldloc add dungeon outpost 12 6
; a duplicate id...
expect-refuse worldloc add dungeon outpost 13 6
; ...and a new id on an occupied cell
expect-refuse worldloc add dungeon gatehouse 10 10
worldloc set outpost dungeon crypt
worldloc set outpost level crypt2
worldloc move outpost 12 7
; and a move onto another doorway
expect-refuse worldloc move outpost 10 10
worldloc

; --- areas: the order rule ---------------------------------------------------
worldarea
worldarea at 6 6
worldarea add tiny 6 6 2 2 0.9
worldarea
; the newest row wins where it overlaps...
worldarea at 6 6
worldarea order tiny 0
worldarea
; ...and moving it FIRST hands the cell back to the broad region above it.
worldarea at 6 6
worldarea del tiny
worldarea

; --- and all of it is one history -------------------------------------------
undo
worldarea

; --- the rules the DIALOG sits on are the same ones ---------------------------
; W4's settings dialog edits through WorldMap::SetStart / AddArea / RemoveArea /
; MoveArea, the same calls these commands make. That is the point of moving the
; refusals into the map: there is one editor wearing two faces, so a rule proved
; here is proved for the dialog too. Three that were only in the console before:
expect-refuse worldarea add lowlands 0 0 4 4
expect-refuse worldarea add flat 3 3 0 4
; (the undo above put tiny back at row 0, so this asks for where it is)
expect-refuse worldarea order tiny 0
worldarea

; --- and the dialog itself opens where its button is, and nowhere else --------
; It is modal over the WORLD screen, the one state whose Update routes input to
; it; opened over a dungeon it would be a modal nothing could close.
worldmap off
expect-refuse worldsettings
worldmap on
worldsettings
worldsettings off

; --- each tab's status row is its own (code-review C102) ---------------------
; Opened on a doorway, as a right-click opens it; then a start refused on the
; World tab (0,0 is water) and a move refused on the Doorways tab (off the
; grid), each through the call its fields make. A refusal must land in the row
; of the tab that raised it and leave the other's alone. With one row shared by
; the tabs, the doorway's form showed the World tab's start caption, and the
; refused start was written into the hidden Doorways row. (`start` and `move`
; print the rows after them, as `status` does.)
worldsettings crypt_gate
worldsettings status
worldsettings start 0 0
worldsettings move 99 99
; ...and a DELETE takes the doorway's note with it. With nothing selected the
; Doorways row stands under "+ Add", and a refusal about a doorway that is gone
; would sit there. A doorway is added for it (selected), refused a move, then
; deleted - which leaves the world as it was. Add and delete rebuild the tab a
; frame later, so the row they leave is read by the `status` after them.
worldsettings add
worldsettings move 99 99
worldsettings delete
worldsettings status
worldsettings off
worldmap off

; --- W5: the picker's two tiers, and where a new level lands ----------------
; The toolbar dropdown is what W5 built; `levels` prints the same grouping
; through the same Project helpers, which is the part a screenshot cannot
; assert. Three facts: every level belongs to a dungeon (the orphan row is the
; control — it reads 0, so the grouping accounts for the whole manifest), a new
; level lands INSIDE the dungeon being viewed, and its stem is named after it.
; --- every project file survives being written, FIRST ------------------------
; BEFORE anything saves, so the writers are diffed against the files as they
; were hand-authored. Run AFTER a save it would be comparing the writer with
; its own output, which agrees with itself however much it drops -- and did:
; deleting the blank-line rule was invisible from here until this moved up.
catround

levels
levels new crypt
levels

; --- W6: the player's map has two pages --------------------------------------
; The M-map can show the overworld while the party is in a dungeon. The state
; machine is the checkable half (the drawing is not): a fresh open starts on
; the DUNGEON, the toggle is only offered when the project has a world, and the
; page cannot outlive the overlay — a stair fires Close() from somewhere that
; knows nothing about pages, and a latched page would leave the TRAVEL screen
; offering a way back to a dungeon the party had left.
; closed, the page is dungeon and the toggle is offered (there IS a world)...
mappage
; ...and setting it to world while the map is shut changes nothing, because the
; page is DERIVED from the overlay being up rather than remembered beside it -
; so it is refused, and still reports the page it left.
expect-refuse mappage world
; opening resets the page, exactly as pressing M does: a fresh map shows
; where you ARE.
mappage open
; and NOW the toggle takes.
mappage world
mappage dungeon
mappage close
; shut again -> dungeon, whatever it was showing.
mappage
